  ],
  "text": "One ship turned sideways in the Suez Canal and held up billions of dollars a day. Almost all world trade goes by sea, and the sea is not open everywhere. It funnels through about six narrow gaps. Suez. Panama. Malacca. Hormuz. Bab-el-Mandeb. The Danish straits. A fifth of container traffic goes through Malacca. A fifth of all oil through Hormuz. And modern supply chains carry almost no slack, deliberately. Just in time means the part arrives the day it is needed, not a month before. That is enormously efficient, and it means there is nothing sitting in reserve. So one blockage in one of those gaps stops a factory somewhere else within days. We built the most efficient system in history by removing every buffer it had. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
